%I A402562 #5 Nov 26 2026 19:56:41
%S A402562 2,3,7,43,60443
%N A402562 Primes p such that Product_{primes q < p} (q-1) == 1 (mod p).
%C A402562 Let p = prime(n). Then Product_{primes q < p} (q-1) = A005867(n-1), so p is a term if and only if p divides A005867(n-1) - 1, or equivalently A005867(n) + 1, since A005867(n) = (p-1)*A005867(n-1).
%C A402562 This is the analog of A341804 with the product of the primes q < p replaced by the product of q-1. The analog of A066735 is A402563.
%C A402562 2 and 3 are terms trivially: for p = 2 the product is empty, and for p = 3 it is 2-1 = 1.
%C A402562 The primes p such that Product_{primes q < p} (1-q) == -1 (mod p) are 2, 3, 7, 31, 43, 60443 (no others below 10^11). Since Product_{primes q < p} (1-q) = (-1)^(pi(p)-1)*Product_{primes q < p} (q-1), where pi = A000720, they are the terms of this sequence with pi(p) even together with the terms of A402563 with pi(p) odd.
%C A402562 Heuristically, if the product behaves like a random nonzero residue modulo p, then p is a term with probability 1/(p-1), and the number of terms below x should grow like log(log(x)), the same heuristic count as for the Wilson primes A007540. This suggests that the sequence is infinite but very sparse.
%C A402562 a(6) > 10^11. The search used an accumulating remainder tree, the method of Costa, Gerbicz and Harvey for Wilson primes (see Links).
%H A402562 Edgar Costa, Robert Gerbicz and David Harvey, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-2014-02800-7">A search for Wilson primes</a>, Mathematics of Computation, Vol. 83, No. 290 (2014), pp. 3071-3091; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1209.3436">arXiv preprint</a>, arXiv:1209.3436 [math.NT], 2012.
%e A402562 7 is a term: (2-1)*(3-1)*(5-1) = 8 = 7 + 1, so the product == 1 (mod 7).
%e A402562 5 is not a term: (2-1)*(3-1) = 2, which is not == 1 (mod 5).
%e A402562 43 is a term: the product of q-1 over the 13 primes q < 43 is A005867(13) = 44144787456000 = 43*1026622964093 + 1.
%o A402562 (PARI) isok(p) = if(!isprime(p), return(0)); my(w=Mod(1, p)); forprime(q=2, p-1, w *= q-1); w == 1;
%o A402562 (PARI) lista(nn) = my(t=1); forprime(p=2, nn, if(t%p == 1, print1(p, ", ")); t *= p-1);
%o A402562 (Python)
%o A402562 from sympy import primerange
%o A402562 def aupto(limit):
%o A402562     t, alst = 1, []
%o A402562     for p in primerange(2, limit+1):
%o A402562         if t % p == 1: alst.append(p)
%o A402562         t *= p - 1
%o A402562     return alst
%o A402562 print(aupto(10**5))
%Y A402562 Cf. A000720, A002110, A005867, A007540, A062347, A066735, A341804, A402563.
%K A402562 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A402562 1,1
%A A402562 _Keith McCurdy_, Nov 21 2026
